title: Datadog Cloud SIEM detection rules, hands-on
guide: howto/datadog-cloud-siem.html
promise: Learn Datadog searches and rules from zero on 24 CloudTrail events, with renamed fields and a threshold that fails silently.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for beginners new to Datadog, a hosted monitoring platform, and to writing detection rules for its Cloud SIEM (security information and event management). Allow two hours.

You need a terminal with curl and jq, and a Datadog organisation.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
No Datadog account was used, so no search or rule ran in Datadog. Every operator and setting was checked against Datadog's documentation as of 27 September 2026, and the results it quotes were worked out by a local script.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. DATADOG blocks are searches for the search bar of Log Explorer, Datadog's page for searching logs; BASH blocks go into a terminal. HCL blocks are optional Terraform rules; Terraform creates resources from text files.

== Set up: an organisation you can test in
section: 1. Get an organisation you can test in
Use a trial or non-production organisation; Datadog offers a 14-day free trial. Create an API (application programming interface) key, the secret that lets your upload command send logs, in organisation settings. Make sure Cloud SIEM analyses logs with source:cloudtrail.

== Set up: load the lab data
section: 2. Load the lab data
Step 2 writes the 24 lab events and moves them so the last is five minutes old. Set DD_SITE to your site, such as datadoghq.com, and read your API key into DD_API_KEY; this sends them. Expect 202.
```bash
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST "https://http-intake.logs.$DD_SITE/api/v2/logs" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H "DD-API-KEY: $DD_API_KEY" --data-binary @upload.json
```

== A rule is a search wrapped in settings
section: A search that selects, a rule that counts
Cloud SIEM records a security signal, an entry for an analyst to review, when a detection rule matches. A rule has two halves: a query, a search that only selects logs, and settings that group, count and time them.

== The lab: 24 CloudTrail events and a leaked key
section: Walk-through
The lab is 24 events from CloudTrail, the record Amazon Web Services (AWS) keeps of actions in an account: who made each call, from which address, and whether AWS refused it.

A continuous integration user's access key, build-bot, has leaked.

== What someone does with build-bot's key
section: Walk-through
From 203.0.113.50 someone is mostly refused, then creates a user, backup-svc, gives it administrator rights (the AdministratorAccess policy) and an access key, and uses it to stop CloudTrail logging.

Ordinary work includes a sign-in without multi-factor authentication (MFA).

== include: _cloudtrail-lab.deck

== Count what arrived, then read the names
section: 3. Find the logs and read their names
Set Log Explorer's time range to the past 4 hours: expect 24. Datadog's CloudTrail pipeline, its processing of each arriving log, maps eventName to @evt.name and errorCode to @error.kind. Adding @evt.name:*, matching logs that have it, should still find 24.
```datadog
source:cloudtrail env:lab
```

== D1: search the names Datadog gives the fields
section: 4. Filter on one attribute: trail tampering (D1)
Datadog's own rules search these names, and @network.client.ip for sourceIPAddress. Attributes, Datadog's word for fields, take an @; source and tags such as env:lab are bare. Parentheses list alternatives, with OR in capitals. Expect one log: StopLogging by backup-svc.
```datadog
source:cloudtrail @evt.name:(StopLogging OR DeleteTrail OR UpdateTrail OR PutEventSelectors)
```

== Before you trust a quiet rule, run a control
section: 6. Counting lives in the rule: the burst that never fires (D2)
Grouped by @network.client.ip, @error.kind:AccessDenied counts 4 for 203.0.113.50 and 3 for 192.0.2.20, so a rule needing 5 never fires. This control, which must find something, grouped by @error.kind, shows EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud) wrote three of 203.0.113.50's refusals as Client.UnauthorizedOperation.
```datadog
source:cloudtrail @error.kind:*
```

== D2: the rule groups, counts and times
section: 6. Counting lives in the rule: the burst that never fires (D2)
Step 9 saves it in main.tf. The group by counts each address apart; the case, a >= 5, is the condition on query a's count; the evaluation window makes each count look back 600 seconds. Expect one signal, for 203.0.113.50.
```hcl
    query           = "source:cloudtrail @error.kind:(AccessDenied OR AccessDeniedException OR Client.UnauthorizedOperation OR UnauthorizedOperation)"
    group_by_fields = ["@network.client.ip"]
  }
  case {
    status    = "medium"
    condition = "a >= 5"
  }
  options {
    evaluation_window   = 600
```

== D3: in a sequence, the window is the detection
section: 8. One user, two events, in order (D3)
Step 9 saves it in main.tf. A Sequence rule's steps come in order: CreateUser, then CreateAccessKey for that new user within the transition's 600 seconds. Expect backup-svc only; at 10800, svc-reporting, keyed 2 hours 37 minutes after creation, appears too.
```hcl
      steps {
        name              = "key_created"
        condition         = "b > 0"
        evaluation_window = 60
      }
      step_transitions {
        parent            = "user_created"
        child             = "key_created"
        evaluation_window = 600 # the "within 10 minutes"
      }
```

== Test each rule both ways
section: 10. Test each rule both ways
Paste processed logs into the rule editor's Unit Test for D1: StopLogging should match and ListBuckets should not. For D2 and D3, choose Run as Historical Job over the past 4 hours: expect 203.0.113.50 and backup-svc, not 192.0.2.20 or svc-reporting.

== Exercise: write the search for D5
section: Hands-on exercises
Write the search for D5, AdministratorAccess attached to a user, and say what the rule around it needs. In the guide, "Show the answer" opens each answer.

== Answer: one query and a case of a > 0
section: Hands-on exercises
Expect one log: build-bot attaching the policy to backup-svc. The ARN (Amazon Resource Name) needs double quotes because it contains colons. The rule needs one query and a case of a > 0, so a single matching log is enough.
```datadog
source:cloudtrail @evt.name:AttachUserPolicy @requestParameters.policyArn:"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess"
```

== Exercise: a sign-in search that finds nothing
section: Hands-on exercises
This search finds nothing, although the lab contains a sign-in without MFA. A dot in a name walks into nested JSON (JavaScript Object Notation). Why does it find nothing, and how do you prove it rather than guess?
```datadog
source:cloudtrail @evt.name:ConsoleLogin @additionalEventData.MFAUsed:no
```

== Answer: values are case sensitive
section: Hands-on exercises
CloudTrail writes No, not no. To prove it, drop the term that decides and group what is left by @additionalEventData.MFAUsed: expect No 2 and Yes 1. Seeing the real values answers "what exactly is it called?"
```datadog
source:cloudtrail @evt.name:ConsoleLogin
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Read a processed log before trusting field names from AWS's documentation.
- List the error codes that exist before choosing which to count.
- Datadog rejects logs dated over 18 hours back, and a 202 only means accepted for processing.
